 "meta": {
  "title": "The painter who handed us his own head",
  "description": "David with the Head of Goliath by Caravaggio (Galleria Borghese, Rome). The severed head is the painter's self-portrait; the picture may have been sent to Cardinal Scipione Borghese, who could grant him a pardon for murder.",
  "tags": [
   "art history",
   "caravaggio",
   "david and goliath",
   "baroque",
   "dark art",
   "painting"
  ],
  "question": "A trophy, or a plea for mercy?"
 },
 "sources": [
  {
   "claim": "The sword in David's hand carries an abbreviated inscription H-AS OS; this has been interpreted as an abbreviation of the Latin phrase humilitas occidit superbiam (\"humility kills pride\").",
   "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/David_with_the_Head_of_Goliath_(Caravaggio,_Rome)",
   "note": "sentence H3"
  },
  {
   "claim": "This bond is further complicated by the fact that Caravaggio has depicted himself as Goliath, while the model for David is il suo Caravaggino (\"his own little Caravaggio\").",
   "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/David_with_the_Head_of_Goliath_(Caravaggio,_Rome)",
   "note": "sentence H4"
  },
  {
   "claim": "On 29 May 1606, Caravaggio killed a young man, possibly unintentionally, resulting in him fleeing Rome with a death sentence hanging over him.",
   "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Caravaggio",
   "note": "sentence H5"
  },
  {
   "claim": "David is perturbed, \"his expression mingling sadness and compassion\".",
   "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/David_with_the_Head_of_Goliath_(Caravaggio,_Rome)",
   "note": "sentence H7"
  },
  {
   "claim": "This painting he may have sent to his patron, the unscrupulous art-loving Cardinal Scipione Borghese, nephew of the pope, who had the power to grant or withhold pardons.",
   "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Caravaggio",
   "note": "sentence H7"
  },
  {
   "claim": "If the painting was a gift to Cardinal Borghese, the papal official with the power to grant Caravaggio a pardon for murder, it can also be interpreted as a personal plea for mercy.",
   "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/David_with_the_Head_of_Goliath_(Caravaggio,_Rome)",
   "note": "sentence H8"
  },
  {
   "claim": "News from Rome encouraged Caravaggio, and in the summer of 1610, he took a boat northwards to receive the pardon, which seemed imminent thanks to his powerful Roman friends.",
   "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Caravaggio",
   "note": "sentence H9"
  }
 ],
